              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p70-q29-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Rearrange as a quadratic in ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "x",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " and require ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\Delta=1-4y^2\\ge0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "; check ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "y=0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " separately.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "The denominator is $x^2+2x+2=(x+1)^2+1>0$ for every real $x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y(x^2+2x+2)&=x+1\\\\yx^2+(2y-1)x+(2y-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $y\\ne0$, this is quadratic in $x$. Real solutions require a non-negative discriminant.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(2y-1)^2-4y(2y-1)\\\\\\Delta&=1-4y^2\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4y^2&\\ge0\\\\y^2&\\le\\frac14\\\\-\\frac12&\\le y\\le\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n\nWhen $y=0$, the equation reduces to $-x-1=0$, giving the valid solution $x=-1$. Thus $0$ is also in the range. The endpoints are attained at $x=-2$ and $x=0$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the range is $\\boxed{\\left[-\\frac12,\\frac12\\right]}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
